-- Singapore Penal Code 1871, s 301 — transferred malice
-- "Culpable homicide by causing the death of a person other than the person
--  whose death was intended"
-- As substituted by the Criminal Law Reform Act 2019 (Act 15 of 2019),
-- w.e.f. 1 January 2020.
--
-- An ISOMORPHIC encoding in INERT STYLE: the verbatim statutory prose rides
-- along as inert string literals (identity for their boolean context — TRUE
-- under AND, FALSE under OR), and only the operative atoms are lifted into
-- named leaves. Read a rule top to bottom and you read the section.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The text
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- 301.—(1) If a person, by doing anything which he intends or knows to be
--          likely to cause death, commits culpable homicide by causing the
--          death of any person whose death he neither intends nor knows himself
--          to be likely to cause, the culpable homicide committed by the
--          offender is of the description of which it would have been if he had
--          caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew
--          himself to be likely to cause.
--                                                     [Act 15 of 2019 wef 01/01/2020]
--
--      (2) To avoid doubt, in the circumstances mentioned in subsection (1),
--          the accused person may rely on any defence or exception in law as
--          though the accused person had caused the death of the person whose
--          death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause.
--                                                     [Act 15 of 2019 wef 01/01/2020]
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The shape of the section
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- Two people are in play throughout, and the section names them only by
-- relative clause. Both subsections do the same thing on two different
-- channels, so the encoding keeps the pairing visible:
--
--   INTENDED  "the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely
--             to cause"
--   ACTUAL    "any person whose death he neither intends nor knows himself to
--             be likely to cause" — i.e. the person he in fact killed
--
--   (1) DESCRIPTION channel — the description borne as against ACTUAL is
--       replaced by the description it WOULD have borne as against INTENDED.
--   (2) DEFENCE channel     — the defences available as against ACTUAL are
--       augmented by those that WOULD have been available as against INTENDED.
--
-- Both are counterfactual substitutions, which is why each channel needs two
-- fields on the record: what the law says apart from this section, and what it
-- would have said in the counterfactual this section points at.
--
-- s 26G (general transferred fault, also inserted by Act 15 of 2019) is
-- expressly disapplied where s 301(1) applies — see s 26G(1). s 301 is the
-- death-specific carve-out, and s 26G(5) is the model for s 301(2).

§ `Penal Code 1871`

§§ `Descriptions of culpable homicide`

-- s 301 does not define the descriptions; it only says WHICH ONE attaches.
-- They come from ss 299–300, and are reproduced here as an ontology only.
@ref Penal Code 1871 ss 299, 300 — s 301 supplies no descriptions of its own
DECLARE `Description of culpable homicide` IS ONE OF
    `murder within the meaning of section 300(a)`
    `murder within the meaning of section 300(b)`
    `murder within the meaning of section 300(c)`
    `murder within the meaning of section 300(d)`
    `culpable homicide not amounting to murder`

§§ `The facts of one killing`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 301 — the operative atoms of subsections (1) and (2)
DECLARE Killing HAS
    -- "by doing anything which he intends or knows to be likely to cause death"
    `intends to cause death`                                                                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `knows to be likely to cause death`                                                          IS A BOOLEAN

    -- "commits culpable homicide by causing the death of any person"
    `culpable homicide by causing the death of any person`                                       IS A BOOLEAN

    -- "whose death he neither intends nor knows himself to be likely to cause"
    `intends the death of the person whose death he in fact causes`                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `knows himself to be likely to cause the death of the person whose death he in fact causes`  IS A BOOLEAN

    -- the description channel of subsection (1)
    `the description apart from this section`
        IS A MAYBE `Description of culpable homicide`
    `the description it would have been if he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`
        IS A `Description of culpable homicide`

    -- the defence channel of subsection (2)
    `defence or exception in law apart from this section`
        IS A BOOLEAN
    `defence or exception in law as though he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`
        IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `301(1) — the circumstances`

-- The antecedent of subsection (1), verbatim. The `...` are the sentence's own
-- commas (asyndetic AND); the `..` inside the first group is its "or"; and the
-- NOT wrapped around the second group IS the "neither … nor".
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 301(1) — the "If …" antecedent
GIVEN k IS A Killing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the circumstances mentioned in subsection (1)` k IF
        "If a person, by doing anything which he"
   ...  (    k's `intends to cause death`
         ..  k's `knows to be likely to cause death` )
   ...  ", commits"
   ...  k's `culpable homicide by causing the death of any person`
   ...  "whose death he neither … nor"
   ...  NOT (    k's `intends the death of the person whose death he in fact causes`
             ..  k's `knows himself to be likely to cause the death of the person whose death he in fact causes` )

§§ `301(1) — the description transfers`

-- The consequent: "the culpable homicide committed by the offender is of the
-- description of which it would have been if he had caused the death of the
-- person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause."
--
-- Apart from this section the answer is a MAYBE, and deliberately so: whether
-- ss 299–300 describe the killing of ACTUAL at all — when every fault element
-- was directed at INTENDED — is precisely the question s 301 exists to close.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 301(1) — the deeming consequent
GIVEN k IS A Killing
GIVETH A MAYBE `Description of culpable homicide`
DECIDE `the description of the culpable homicide committed by the offender` k IS
    IF   `the circumstances mentioned in subsection (1)` k
    THEN JUST (k's `the description it would have been if he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`)
    ELSE      k's `the description apart from this section`

§§ `301(2) — the defences transfer`

-- Subsection (2) verbatim. It is a conditional grant, not a substitution: it
-- says what the accused MAY rely on once the subsection (1) circumstances are
-- made out, and says nothing about what he could rely on anyway.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 301(2) — cf. s 26G(5), the general form of the same rule
GIVEN k IS A Killing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accused person may rely on a defence under subsection (2)` k IF
        "To avoid doubt, in"
   ...  `the circumstances mentioned in subsection (1)` k
   ...  "the accused person may rely on any"
   ...  k's `defence or exception in law as though he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`

-- NOT part of s 301. "May rely on" is additive, so the defences actually open
-- to the accused are those he had apart from the section, together with those
-- subsection (2) hands him.
GIVEN k IS A Killing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a defence or exception in law is available to the accused person` k IF
        k's `defence or exception in law apart from this section`
    OR  `the accused person may rely on a defence under subsection (2)` k

§§ `Illustrations`

-- (a) A fires at B intending to cause B's death. The shot misses B and kills C,
--     a bystander whose death A neither intends nor knows himself to be likely
--     to cause. Had B died, the killing of B would have been murder within the
--     meaning of s 300(a). By s 301(1) the killing of C bears that description.
`A shoots at B and kills C` MEANS Killing WITH
    `intends to cause death`                                                                    IS TRUE
    `knows to be likely to cause death`                                                         IS FALSE
    `culpable homicide by causing the death of any person`                                      IS TRUE
    `intends the death of the person whose death he in fact causes`                             IS FALSE
    `knows himself to be likely to cause the death of the person whose death he in fact causes` IS FALSE
    `the description apart from this section`                                                   IS NOTHING
    `the description it would have been if he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`
        IS `murder within the meaning of section 300(a)`
    `defence or exception in law apart from this section`                                       IS FALSE
    `defence or exception in law as though he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`
        IS FALSE

-- (b) The same facts, save that A fired at B in the exercise of A's right of
--     private defence against B. The defence would have availed A had B died,
--     so by s 301(2) A may rely on it on the charge in respect of C — even
--     though A had no right of private defence against C.
`A shoots at B in private defence and kills C` MEANS Killing WITH
    `intends to cause death`                                                                    IS TRUE
    `knows to be likely to cause death`                                                         IS FALSE
    `culpable homicide by causing the death of any person`                                      IS TRUE
    `intends the death of the person whose death he in fact causes`                             IS FALSE
    `knows himself to be likely to cause the death of the person whose death he in fact causes` IS FALSE
    `the description apart from this section`                                                   IS NOTHING
    `the description it would have been if he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`
        IS `murder within the meaning of section 300(a)`
    `defence or exception in law apart from this section`                                       IS FALSE
    `defence or exception in law as though he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`
        IS TRUE

-- (c) A throws a stone into a crowd knowing himself to be likely to cause the
--     death of any of them, and kills C. The "neither … nor" limb fails: A did
--     know himself likely to cause C's death. s 301 has no work to do, and the
--     ordinary law describes the killing of C directly — here, murder within
--     the meaning of s 300(d).
`A stones a crowd and kills C` MEANS Killing WITH
    `intends to cause death`                                                                    IS FALSE
    `knows to be likely to cause death`                                                         IS TRUE
    `culpable homicide by causing the death of any person`                                      IS TRUE
    `intends the death of the person whose death he in fact causes`                             IS FALSE
    `knows himself to be likely to cause the death of the person whose death he in fact causes` IS TRUE
    `the description apart from this section`
        IS JUST `murder within the meaning of section 300(d)`
    `the description it would have been if he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`
        IS `culpable homicide not amounting to murder`
    `defence or exception in law apart from this section`                                       IS FALSE
    `defence or exception in law as though he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`
        IS FALSE

-- (d) The same facts as (b), save that A fired into a group knowing himself to
--     be likely to cause C's death as well as B's. The "neither … nor" limb
--     fails, so subsection (1) is not engaged and subsection (2) hands A
--     nothing: A's right of private defence against B does not reach the charge
--     in respect of C through this section.
`A shoots at B in private defence knowing C is at risk` MEANS Killing WITH
    `intends to cause death`                                                                    IS TRUE
    `knows to be likely to cause death`                                                         IS FALSE
    `culpable homicide by causing the death of any person`                                      IS TRUE
    `intends the death of the person whose death he in fact causes`                             IS FALSE
    `knows himself to be likely to cause the death of the person whose death he in fact causes` IS TRUE
    `the description apart from this section`
        IS JUST `murder within the meaning of section 300(d)`
    `the description it would have been if he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`
        IS `murder within the meaning of section 300(a)`
    `defence or exception in law apart from this section`                                       IS FALSE
    `defence or exception in law as though he had caused the death of the person whose death he intended or knew himself to be likely to cause`
        IS TRUE

§§ `Directives`

#EVAL `the circumstances mentioned in subsection (1)` `A shoots at B and kills C`
#EVAL `the description of the culpable homicide committed by the offender` `A shoots at B and kills C`
#EVAL `a defence or exception in law is available to the accused person` `A shoots at B and kills C`

#EVAL `the accused person may rely on a defence under subsection (2)` `A shoots at B in private defence and kills C`
#EVAL `a defence or exception in law is available to the accused person` `A shoots at B in private defence and kills C`

#EVAL `the circumstances mentioned in subsection (1)` `A shoots at B in private defence knowing C is at risk`
#EVAL `a defence or exception in law is available to the accused person` `A shoots at B in private defence knowing C is at risk`

#EVAL `the circumstances mentioned in subsection (1)` `A stones a crowd and kills C`
#EVAL `the description of the culpable homicide committed by the offender` `A stones a crowd and kills C`

-- (a) the transfer bites: C's killing takes B's description
#ASSERT `the circumstances mentioned in subsection (1)` `A shoots at B and kills C`
#ASSERT `the description of the culpable homicide committed by the offender` `A shoots at B and kills C` EQUALS JUST `murder within the meaning of section 300(a)`

-- (a) but the section transfers no defence that was never there
#ASSERT NOT (`a defence or exception in law is available to the accused person` `A shoots at B and kills C`)

-- (b) s 301(2): the counterfactual defence is available on the actual charge
#ASSERT `a defence or exception in law is available to the accused person` `A shoots at B in private defence and kills C`

-- (c) the "neither … nor" limb fails, so s 301 does not engage and the
--     description apart from the section stands
#ASSERT NOT (`the circumstances mentioned in subsection (1)` `A stones a crowd and kills C`)
#ASSERT `the description of the culpable homicide committed by the offender` `A stones a crowd and kills C` EQUALS JUST `murder within the meaning of section 300(d)`

-- (d) the gate does the work: without the subsection (1) circumstances,
--     subsection (2) transfers nothing, even though the same defence would
--     have availed as against the person whose death A intended
#ASSERT NOT (`the circumstances mentioned in subsection (1)` `A shoots at B in private defence knowing C is at risk`)
#ASSERT NOT (`a defence or exception in law is available to the accused person` `A shoots at B in private defence knowing C is at risk`)
